From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: local MLDs adopt the credentials of a controller without MLD config

A controller that sends no AP MLD Config TLV (EasyMesh R3) still owns the
credentials. 997 made sure such a controller can no longer delete the
node's own MLD configuration; this patch makes that configuration carry what
the controller asked for, and keeps the controller's M2s off MLD links they
do not belong to.

Measured 2026-09-17 on BPI-8g-corridor. The node had been onboarded by our
R6 controller, so map_genconfig had written:

  apmld0  id=2  ap-mld-1    fronthaul  ssid 'Drak2'   sae-mixed  band 2 5 6
  apmld1  id=3  ap-mld-2    backhaul   ssid MAP--BH   sae        band 5 6
  stamld0 id=4  bsta-mld-3  station    ssid MAP--BH              band 5 6

and wlan0/wlan1/wlan2 (bss_index 0) carry mld_id=2. It was then onboarded
by a MediaTek EasyMesh R3 controller, which sends four M2s per radio:

  #0  Multi-AP-1  backhaul   auth 0x01E0 (WPA2-PSK SAE DPP AKM24)
  #1  Multi-AP-2  fronthaul  auth 0x0160 (WPA2-PSK SAE AKM24)
  #2  Multi-AP-3  fronthaul  auth 0x0160
  #3  Multi-AP-4  fronthaul  auth 0x0160

and no AP MLD Config TLV. After handle_ap_autoconfig_wsc():

  wlan0/1/2       ssid Multi-AP-1, type backhaul, mld_id=2 still set
  wlanN-1..3      Multi-AP-2/3/4, fronthaul, no mld_id, BSSes of their own
  wireless        ap_mld_1 still 'Drak2', ap_mld_2 still MAP--BH

so the controller's backhaul network never went on the air, and the old
fronthaul and backhaul networks, which the new controller knows nothing
about, stayed on the air.

Why:

 - wifi_teardown_map_ifaces_by_radio() marks every section on the radio
   torndown. For M2 #0, wifi_get_available_ifname_with_ssid() finds no
   section named Multi-AP-1 and wifi_get_first_available_ifname() returns
   the first torndown one, wlan0. Neither function looks at mld_id, and
   uci_apply_wps_credentials() does not touch it, so the backhaul
   credential is written into a link of the fronthaul MLD.
 - The M2s for #1..#3 find nothing torndown left and agent_gen_netif_ap()
   creates wlanN-1..3. For those, mld_serves_network() asks whether an
   ap_mld wifi-iface with the same SSID covers the radio; ap_mld_1 still
   says 'Drak2', so each gets a per-radio wireless section.
 - With the TLV missing, the handler calls mlo_process_ap_mld_config()
   with num_mlds=0, which 997 turned into a no-op on purpose, so the MLD
   sections keep the old credentials for good.

What this does, only when the M2 set for a radio carries no AP MLD Config
TLV, the radio is MLO capable and the node has MLDs (the same condition the
teardown branch already used):

 1. Before the M2 loop, decode the M2s once more (wsc_process_m2() does not
    change the key material) and choose one credential per class: the one
    whose SSID the MLD already carries, otherwise the first of the class.
    The fronthaul AP MLD takes the fronthaul credential, the backhaul AP
    MLD the backhaul one - mapagent 'mld' section (ssid, key, encryption),
    the in-memory credential, and the wireless wifi-iface of the MLD
    (ifname ap-mld-N). The bSTA MLD takes the backhaul credential only when
    eth_onboards_wifi_bhs is set - the same gate uci_apply_wps_credentials()
    uses for the per-radio bSTAs; otherwise the bSTA keeps what its own
    onboarding gave it. Nothing is written when the values already match,
    so the second and third radio, and a repeated M2, change nothing.
    The wifi-mld 'mldN' sections are left alone: map_genconfig does not
    name them by id (mld0 is ap-mld-1, mld2 is bsta-mld-3).
    This has to happen before the loop: mld_serves_network() reads the
    SSID of ap_mld_N when it decides whether the fronthaul BSS is an MLD
    link or a BSS of its own.

 2. In the M2 loop, the interface is picked by m2_pick_ap(): an AP that
    carries an mld_id takes only the credential of its own MLD (type and
    SSID must match), and is preferred for it; then a section that already
    has the SSID; then a section of the same type; then one with no
    configuration. A torndown section of the other type is not reused -
    a new interface is generated, so a former fronthaul wireless section
    never becomes a second backhaul BSS next to the backhaul MLD. There is
    deliberately no fallback to wifi_get_first_available_ifname(): that is
    the MLD link the credential has just been refused for.

 3. The no-TLV branch still calls mlo_process_ap_mld_config() with
    num_mlds=0 (a no-op since 997) to schedule the reload; only its log
    line changes, because nothing is torn down.

Expected result for the MediaTek case: ap-mld-1 advertises Multi-AP-2 on
2.4/5/6 GHz with wlan0/1/2 as its links, ap-mld-2 advertises Multi-AP-1,
Multi-AP-3/4 get per-radio BSSes, the backhaul M2 gets a new wlanN-M
section without a wireless section of its own (mld_backhaul=1, as before),
and 'Drak2' and MAP--BH are gone from the air.

Unchanged:

 - Our R6 controller sends the AP MLD Config TLV, so adopt_mld_creds is
   false and the old interface choice and mlo_process_ap_mld_config()
   run exactly as before.
 - A TLV with num_mlds=0 is present, so the same holds; 997 keeps it a
   no-op. Nothing is torn down in either case.
 - Nodes without MLDs and radios without AP MLO support.

Needs the matching map_genconfig change: its map_credentials() takes the
last complete fronthaul section and its affiliation loop puts mld_id=2 on
every fronthaul section, which would move Multi-AP-4 onto ap-mld-1 at the
next wifi event and make Multi-AP-3/4 links of it.

Not changed here (#11): auth 0x0160 is WPA2-PSK|SAE|AKM24 (0x0020 is
WPA2-PSK, DPP is 0x0080), so 'sae-mixed' is the faithful mapping. What
get_encryption_value() drops is the AKM24 (SAE-EXT-KEY) bit.

A node that already went through the faulty autoconfiguration can keep
its state while the controller resends the same M2s (DIFF_NONE); test it
from the pre-onboarding R6 configuration, not from the broken one.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
v2 (2026-09-17): the adoption pass decodes a private copy of each M2.
wsc_process_m2() decrypts Encrypted Settings in place; v1 decoded the TLV
itself, so the M2 loop then saw cleartext, logged "Message M2 authentication
failed" and tore the radio down (measured on hardware with our own
controller).

v3 (2026-09-17): a 6 GHz M2 no longer sets the encryption of an MLD that
also has 2.4/5 GHz links; with our own controller the MLD otherwise went
sae (radio2) -> sae-mixed (radio1) on every forced apply.

v4 (2026-09-17), after review:
 - Only the MLD's primary radio changes the MLD: the lowest band of
   mld->band that is not 6 GHz and has an AP MLO radio taking M2s (2.4
   before 5), 6 GHz for a 6 GHz only MLD. Other radios, and radios whose
   band the MLD does not span, never change it, so the result no longer
   depends on the order the radios are configured in. When the primary
   radio changes an MLD, the other already configured radios of that MLD
   are renewed (hash set to a value no M2 set has, in memory and file),
   because their links followed the old SSID.
 - A credential is adopted only if uci_apply_wps_credentials() would write
   it (SSID not empty, known encryption other than 'invalid', band 2/5/6),
   its vendor extension decodes, it is enabled, and it is not placed on a
   bridge other than the AL bridge; the first such credential of the class
   is taken when none carries the MLD's SSID. A decode or extension error
   or a teardown bit in any M2 adopts nothing (the M2 loop handles those).
   An MLD link accepts only such a credential as well.
 - After the M2 loop, a link of an AP MLD spanning this radio that no M2
   went to is kept as it is instead of being deleted (and re-added by
   map_genconfig at the next wifi event).
 - An mld_id that names no AP MLD counts as no MLD.
 - The ext/vendor IEs are freed when no interface can be found.

--- a/src/agent_map.c
+++ b/src/agent_map.c
@@ -2837,6 +2837,500 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+#if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
+/* Credential class of a WSC M2, from the Multi-AP extension bits. Same
+ * encoding as uci_apply_wps_credentials(): 1 backhaul, 2 fronthaul,
+ * 3 combined, 0 is treated as fronthaul there as well.
+ */
+static uint8_t m2_multi_ap(const struct wps_credential *out)
+{
+	return (BIT1(5, out->mapie) << 1) | BIT1(6, out->mapie);
+}
+
+static bool mld_type_takes_m2(enum mld_type type, uint8_t multi_ap)
+{
+	if (multi_ap == 0x01)
+		return type == MLD_TYPE_BACKHAUL_BSS;
+	if (multi_ap == 0x03)
+		return type == MLD_TYPE_COMBINED_BSS;
+
+	return type == MLD_TYPE_FRONTHAUL_BSS;
+}
+
+/* The AP MLD an AP is a link of, or NULL. An mld_id that names no MLD, or
+ * names the bSTA MLD, is treated as no MLD at all.
+ */
+static struct mld_credential *m2_ap_mld(struct agent *a, struct netif_ap *ap)
+{
+	struct mld_credential *mld;
+
+	if (!ap->cfg || !ap->cfg->mld_id)
+		return NULL;
+
+	mld = agent_get_mld_credential_by_id(&a->cfg, ap->cfg->mld_id);
+	if (!mld)
+		return NULL;
+
+	if (mld->type != MLD_TYPE_FRONTHAUL_BSS &&
+	    mld->type != MLD_TYPE_BACKHAUL_BSS &&
+	    mld->type != MLD_TYPE_COMBINED_BSS)
+		return NULL;
+
+	return mld;
+}
+
+/* The one radio band that may change an MLD's ssid, key and encryption:
+ * the lowest band of the MLD that is not 6 GHz (2.4 before 5), or 6 GHz
+ * for a 6 GHz only MLD. A band counts only when this node has an AP MLO
+ * capable radio on it that takes M2s (not a dedicated backhaul) -
+ * otherwise no radio would ever be primary and the MLD would never follow
+ * the controller. All of this is configuration, not arrival order.
+ *
+ * Every radio gets its own M2 set, in no fixed order. Letting each of them
+ * change the MLD made the result depend on that order: a controller with
+ * per-band SSIDs flipped the MLD with every autoconfiguration, and 6 GHz
+ * ('sae') and 2.4/5 GHz ('sae-mixed') took turns on the encryption. The
+ * other radios only follow what the primary one decided.
+ */
+static enum wifi_band mld_primary_band(struct agent *a,
+				       const struct mld_credential *mld)
+{
+	static const enum wifi_band order[] = { BAND_2, BAND_5, BAND_6 };
+	struct wifi_radio_element *re = NULL;
+	size_t i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(order); i++) {
+		if (!(mld->band & order[i]))
+			continue;
+
+		list_for_each_entry(re, &a->radiolist, list) {
+			if (re->band == order[i] && !re->dedicated_backhaul &&
+			    agent_radio_support_ap_mlo(&re->wifi7_caps))
+				return order[i];
+		}
+	}
+
+	return BAND_UNKNOWN;
+}
+
+/* One decoded M2 of the set, as far as the adoption needs it. */
+struct m2_cand {
+	struct wps_credential cred;
+	uint8_t cls;
+	bool usable;		/* may be adopted into an MLD */
+};
+
+/* Would this credential be written as it is? Everything that
+ * uci_apply_wps_credentials() refuses, or that would put a network on
+ * the MLD which the M2 itself does not put on the air, is left out.
+ *
+ * Bridge: the MLD is one netdev on the node's main bridge (map_genconfig
+ * puts ap-mld-N on the AL bridge). A credential that the controller
+ * places on another bridge is a separate network; it stays a BSS of its
+ * own instead of dragging the MLD, with all of its links, onto a bridge
+ * the MLD is not on. VLANs are not in the extension - they come from the
+ * Traffic Separation Policy, keyed by SSID, and are not decided here.
+ */
+static bool m2_cand_usable(struct agent *a, const struct wps_credential *cred,
+			   const struct wsc_ext *exts)
+{
+	char enc[32] = {0};
+	int mfp = 0;
+
+	if (cred->ssidlen == 0 || cred->ssidlen > sizeof(cred->ssid) ||
+	    cred->keylen > sizeof(cred->key))
+		return false;
+
+	/* the only bands uci_apply_wps_credentials() writes */
+	if (cred->band != BAND_2 && cred->band != BAND_5 &&
+	    cred->band != BAND_6)
+		return false;
+
+	if (!get_encryption_value(cred->auth_type, cred->enc_type,
+				  enc, sizeof(enc), &mfp) ||
+	    !strlen(enc) || !strncmp(enc, "invalid", 7))
+		return false;
+
+	if (!exts->enabled)
+		return false;
+
+#ifdef VENDOR_EXTENSION
+	if (exts->bridge[0]) {
+		const char *main_br = a->cfg.al_bridge;
+		const char *ext_br = exts->bridge;
+		char br[sizeof(exts->bridge) + 1] = {0};
+
+		memcpy(br, ext_br, sizeof(exts->bridge));
+		ext_br = br;
+		if (!strncmp(ext_br, "br-", 3))
+			ext_br += 3;
+		if (!strncmp(main_br, "br-", 3))
+			main_br += 3;
+		if (strcmp(ext_br, main_br))
+			return false;
+	}
+#endif
+
+	return true;
+}
+
+/* Adopt a controller credential into a local AP MLD (or the bSTA MLD).
+ *
+ * Writes the mapagent 'mld' section, the in-memory credential and, for an
+ * AP MLD, the wireless wifi-iface that carries the MLD (ifname ap-mld-N -
+ * the one map_genconfig writes). The wifi-mld 'mldN' sections are left
+ * alone: their names do not follow the id (map_genconfig has mld0 for
+ * ap-mld-1 and mld2 for bsta-mld-3), so writing by id would overwrite
+ * the wrong one.
+ *
+ * Returns true when anything changed. Comparing first keeps it idempotent:
+ * a repeated M2 set changes nothing.
+ */
+static bool mld_adopt_m2_credential(struct agent *a, struct mld_credential *mld,
+				    const struct wps_credential *cred,
+				    enum wifi_band radio_band)
+{
+	char ssid[33] = {0};
+	char key[65] = {0};
+	char enc[32] = {0};
+	char idstr[4] = {0};
+	int mfp = 0;
+
+	memcpy(ssid, cred->ssid, cred->ssidlen > 32 ? 32 : cred->ssidlen);
+	memcpy(key, cred->key, cred->keylen > 64 ? 64 : cred->keylen);
+	if (!get_encryption_value(cred->auth_type, cred->enc_type,
+				  enc, sizeof(enc), &mfp))
+		return false;
+
+	/* 6 GHz allows SAE only, so its M2 says "sae" where the 2.4/5 GHz M2s
+	 * of the same network say "sae-mixed". Only the primary radio gets
+	 * here, and that is 6 GHz only when the MLD has no usable 2.4/5 GHz
+	 * radio, so this rarely fires. It keeps an encryption the MLD
+	 * already has for its 2.4/5 GHz links rather than narrowing them to
+	 * SAE only.
+	 */
+	if ((radio_band & BAND_6) && (mld->band & (BAND_2 | BAND_5)) &&
+	    strlen(mld->encryption))
+		snprintf(enc, sizeof(enc), "%s", mld->encryption);
+
+	if (!strncmp((char *)mld->ssid, ssid, 32) &&
+	    !strncmp((char *)mld->key, key, 64) &&
+	    !strncmp(mld->encryption, enc, sizeof(mld->encryption)))
+		return false;
+
+	if (mld->type == MLD_TYPE_BACKHAUL_STA) {
+		struct netif_bkcfg *bk = NULL;
+
+		/* Same gate as the per-radio bSTAs in
+		 * uci_apply_wps_credentials(): without it the bSTA takes its
+		 * credentials from its own onboarding, not from an M2.
+		 */
+		if (!a->cfg.eth_onboards_wifi_bhs)
+			return false;
+
+		list_for_each_entry(bk, &a->cfg.bklist, list) {
+			if (!bk->is_mld_netdev || bk->mld_id != mld->id)
+				continue;
+
+			if (wifi_apply_iface_cfg(bk->name, enc, ssid, key))
+				continue;
+
+			strncpy(bk->ssid, ssid, sizeof(bk->ssid) - 1);
+			strncpy(bk->key, key, sizeof(bk->key) - 1);
+			strncpy(bk->encryption, enc, sizeof(bk->encryption) - 1);
+		}
+	} else if (strlen(mld->ifname)) {
+		uci_set_wireless_interface_option("wireless", "wifi-iface",
+						  "ifname", mld->ifname,
+						  "ssid", ssid);
+		uci_set_wireless_interface_option("wireless", "wifi-iface",
+						  "ifname", mld->ifname,
+						  "key", key);
+		uci_set_wireless_interface_option("wireless", "wifi-iface",
+						  "ifname", mld->ifname,
+						  "encryption", enc);
+	}
+
+	snprintf(idstr, sizeof(idstr), "%u", mld->id);
+	uci_set_wireless_interface_option("mapagent", "mld", "id", idstr,
+					  "ssid", ssid);
+	uci_set_wireless_interface_option("mapagent", "mld", "id", idstr,
+					  "key", key);
+	uci_set_wireless_interface_option("mapagent", "mld", "id", idstr,
+					  "encryption", enc);
+
+	agnt_info(LOG_APCFG, "%s: MLD %u (%s) '%s' -> '%s' %s\n", __func__,
+		  mld->id, mld->ifname, (char *)mld->ssid, ssid, enc);
+
+	memset(mld->ssid, 0, sizeof(mld->ssid));
+	memset(mld->key, 0, sizeof(mld->key));
+	memset(mld->encryption, 0, sizeof(mld->encryption));
+	strncpy((char *)mld->ssid, ssid, sizeof(mld->ssid) - 1);
+	strncpy((char *)mld->key, key, sizeof(mld->key) - 1);
+	strncpy(mld->encryption, enc, sizeof(mld->encryption) - 1);
+
+	return true;
+}
+
+/* An MLD changed on its primary radio. Radios of the MLD that were already
+ * configured followed the old credential (their links took only an M2
+ * with the old SSID) - make them ask again. The hash is set to a value no
+ * M2 set hashes to, in memory and in the file, so the repeated M2 set is
+ * applied rather than skipped as DIFF_NONE, also across a restart; an
+ * all-zero hash would instead be rebuilt from UCI. Radios still waiting for
+ * their M2 are left alone: a new M1 would orphan the M2 in flight, and
+ * that M2 already meets the new credential.
+ */
+static void mld_renew_other_radios(struct agent *a,
+				   const struct mld_credential *mld,
+				   struct wifi_radio_element *primary)
+{
+	struct wifi_radio_element *re = NULL;
+	bool trigger = false;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(re, &a->radiolist, list) {
+		if (re == primary || !(re->band & mld->band))
+			continue;
+		if (re->dedicated_backhaul || re->state != AUTOCFG_HEARTBEAT)
+			continue;
+
+		agnt_dbg(LOG_APCFG, "%s: MLD %u changed on %s, renew %s\n",
+			 __func__, mld->id, primary->name, re->name);
+		memset(re->autconfig.sha256, 0xff, SHA256_LENGTH);
+		autoconfig_radio_hash_to_file(re->macaddr, re->autconfig.sha256);
+		re->state = AUTOCFG_ACTIVE;
+		trigger = true;
+	}
+
+	if (trigger)
+		agent_trigger_autocfg(a);
+}
+
+/* A controller that sends no AP MLD Config TLV (EasyMesh R3 and older) still
+ * owns the credentials. The local MLDs - generated by map_genconfig, and
+ * protected from teardown by 997 - take the controller's credentials by type,
+ * so that what goes on the air is what the controller asked for.
+ *
+ * Only on the MLD's primary radio (mld_primary_band()). There, of the usable
+ * M2s of the MLD's class (m2_cand_usable()), the one whose SSID the MLD
+ * already carries wins, otherwise the first one. Other radios leave the MLD
+ * as it is; their links follow it in m2_pick_ap().
+ *
+ * The M2s are decoded once more here, each from a private copy:
+ * wsc_process_m2() decrypts the Encrypted Settings in place, so decoding the
+ * TLV itself would leave the M2 loop below with cleartext it then fails to
+ * authenticate ("Message M2 authentication failed" -> teardown). An M2 the
+ * loop will refuse (decode or extension error, teardown bit) makes this
+ * adopt nothing at all.
+ *
+ * Must run before the M2 loop: uci_apply_wps_credentials() asks the wireless
+ * AP MLD section for the SSID when it decides whether a fronthaul BSS is a
+ * link of the MLD or a BSS of its own.
+ */
+static bool m2_adopt_local_mlds(struct agent *a, struct wifi_radio_element *radio,
+				struct tlv *tv[][TLV_MAXNUM])
+{
+	struct m2_cand *cand;
+	struct mld_credential *mld = NULL;
+	bool changed = false;
+	int num = 0;
+	int n;
+
+	cand = calloc(TLV_MAXNUM, sizeof(*cand));
+	if (!cand)
+		return false;
+
+	while (num < TLV_MAXNUM && tv[AP_AUTOCONFIGURATION_WSC_M2_WSC_IDX][num]) {
+		struct wps_credential out = {0};
+		struct wsc_ext exts = {
+			.enabled = true
+		};
+		uint8_t *ext = NULL;
+		uint16_t extlen = 0;
+		int ret;
+
+		uint16_t m2len = tlv_length(tv[AP_AUTOCONFIGURATION_WSC_M2_WSC_IDX][num]);
+		uint8_t *m2copy = malloc(m2len);
+
+		if (!m2copy)
+			goto out;
+		memcpy(m2copy, tv[AP_AUTOCONFIGURATION_WSC_M2_WSC_IDX][num]->data, m2len);
+		ret = wsc_process_m2(radio->autconfig.m1_frame,
+				     radio->autconfig.m1_size,
+				     radio->autconfig.key,
+				     m2copy, m2len,
+				     &out, &ext, &extlen);
+		free(m2copy);
+
+		/* The M2 loop reports and handles these; adopt nothing. */
+		if (!ret)
+			ret = wsc_get_exts(ext, extlen, &exts);
+		if (ext)
+			free(ext);
+		agent_free_wsc_vendor_ies(&exts);
+		if (ret || BIT1(4, out.mapie)) {
+			memset(&out, 0, sizeof(out));
+			goto out;
+		}
+
+		cand[num].cred = out;
+		cand[num].cls = m2_multi_ap(&out) ? m2_multi_ap(&out) : 0x02;
+		cand[num].usable = m2_cand_usable(a, &out, &exts);
+		memset(&out, 0, sizeof(out));
+		num++;
+	}
+
+	list_for_each_entry(mld, &a->cfg.mldlist, list) {
+		struct wps_credential *pick = NULL;
+		uint8_t cls;
+
+		if (mld->type == MLD_TYPE_BACKHAUL_STA)
+			cls = 0x01;
+		else if (mld->type == MLD_TYPE_BACKHAUL_BSS)
+			cls = 0x01;
+		else if (mld->type == MLD_TYPE_FRONTHAUL_BSS)
+			cls = 0x02;
+		else
+			continue;
+
+		if (!(mld->band & radio->band) ||
+		    mld_primary_band(a, mld) != radio->band)
+			continue;
+
+		for (n = 0; n < num; n++) {
+			if (!cand[n].usable || cand[n].cls != cls)
+				continue;
+
+			if (cand[n].cred.ssidlen == strlen((char *)mld->ssid) &&
+			    !memcmp(mld->ssid, cand[n].cred.ssid,
+				    cand[n].cred.ssidlen)) {
+				pick = &cand[n].cred;
+				break;
+			}
+
+			if (!pick)
+				pick = &cand[n].cred;
+		}
+
+		if (!pick)
+			continue;
+
+		if (mld_adopt_m2_credential(a, mld, pick, radio->band)) {
+			changed = true;
+			if (mld->type != MLD_TYPE_BACKHAUL_STA)
+				mld_renew_other_radios(a, mld, radio);
+		}
+	}
+
+out:
+	memset(cand, 0, TLV_MAXNUM * sizeof(*cand));
+	free(cand);
+	return changed;
+}
+
+/* May this torndown AP take this M2? An affiliated AP takes only the
+ * credential of its own MLD: matching class, the MLD's SSID, and nothing
+ * the MLD itself would not have adopted (disabled, another bridge).
+ * Measured 2026-09-17: the backhaul M2 of a MediaTek R3 controller landed
+ * on wlan0/1/2, which carry mld_id of the fronthaul MLD, so the backhaul
+ * SSID became a fronthaul link.
+ */
+static bool m2_ap_accepts(struct agent *a, struct netif_ap *ap,
+			  const char *ssid, uint8_t multi_ap, bool usable)
+{
+	struct mld_credential *mld = m2_ap_mld(a, ap);
+
+	if (!mld)
+		return true;
+
+	if (!usable)
+		return false;
+
+	if (!mld_type_takes_m2(mld->type, multi_ap ? multi_ap : 0x02))
+		return false;
+
+	return !strncmp((char *)mld->ssid, ssid, 32);
+}
+
+/* Interface choice for an M2 when the local MLDs have adopted the
+ * controller's credentials. Preference: a link of the MLD this credential
+ * belongs to, then a section already carrying the SSID, then a section of
+ * the same type, then one without configuration. A section of the other
+ * type is not reused - a fresh interface is generated instead, so a former
+ * fronthaul wireless section never turns into a second backhaul next to
+ * the backhaul MLD.
+ */
+static struct netif_ap *m2_pick_ap(struct agent *a, struct wifi_radio_element *re,
+				   const char *ssid, uint8_t multi_ap,
+				   bool usable, char *ifname)
+{
+	struct netif_ap *ap = NULL, *best = NULL;
+	int best_rank = 0;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(ap, &re->aplist, list) {
+		int rank;
+
+		if (!ap->torndown)
+			continue;
+
+		if (ap->cfg && ap->cfg->placeholder)
+			continue;
+
+		if (!m2_ap_accepts(a, ap, ssid, multi_ap, usable))
+			continue;
+
+		if (!ap->cfg)
+			rank = 1;
+		else if (m2_ap_mld(a, ap))
+			rank = 4;
+		else if (!strncmp(ap->cfg->ssid, ssid, 32))
+			rank = 3;
+		else if (ap->cfg->multi_ap == (multi_ap ? multi_ap : 0x02))
+			rank = 2;
+		else
+			continue;
+
+		if (rank > best_rank) {
+			best = ap;
+			best_rank = rank;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (best)
+		strncpy(ifname, best->ifname, IFNAMSIZ - 1);
+
+	return best;
+}
+
+/* After the M2 loop: a link of an AP MLD on this radio that no M2 went to
+ * stays as it is. The set had no credential for it - no M2 of the MLD's
+ * class, or none with the MLD's SSID because the controller names the
+ * network differently on this band. Removing the link would only take it
+ * out of the MLD until map_genconfig puts it back at the next wifi event,
+ * and out again at the next M2.
+ */
+static void m2_keep_unclaimed_mld_links(struct agent *a,
+					struct wifi_radio_element *re)
+{
+	struct netif_ap *ap = NULL;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(ap, &re->aplist, list) {
+		struct mld_credential *mld;
+
+		if (!ap->torndown)
+			continue;
+
+		mld = m2_ap_mld(a, ap);
+		if (!mld || !(mld->band & re->band))
+			continue;
+
+		agnt_dbg(LOG_APCFG, "%s: %s: no M2 for '%s' (MLD %u), link kept\n",
+			 __func__, ap->ifname, (char *)mld->ssid, mld->id);
+		ap->torndown = false;
+	}
+}
